<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manetho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. early 3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manetho was an Egyptian priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> who wrote a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in Greek, the Aegyptiaca, under the early Ptolemies in the 3rd century BCE. His division of Egyptian kings into thirty dynasties is still the basis of Egyptian chronology. The work itself is lost and survives only in excerpts and king lists quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and by the Christian chroniclers Africanus and Eusebius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manetho.jpg" alt="Manetho" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Waddell, W. G. (William Gillan), 1884-1945 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manetho_Waddell1940_PD_Excerpt_pIX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
